Rust-Baroque

Creative Catalyst: Nomadic desert traders salvage crushed synthesizers from dune-buried ruins to power a frantic, digitized insurgency.

This genre is a violent clash between the abrasive, anti-establishment ethos of punk and the crystalline, logic-gate precision of chiptune, played on instruments that shouldn't survive the heat. The Moog Minimoog acts as the "anchor of the sands," its thick, buttery oscillators shoved through rusted signal chains to sound like shredding metal. It features erratic, high-velocity arrangements that feel like a glitching map of a crumbling empire, characterized by biting, square-wave guitar distortion and melodic arpeggios that snap like dry twigs.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 160–190 BPM, jittery and off-kilter, shifting frequently between double-time punk thrash and mechanical, stilted chiptune micro-rhythms.
*   Harmonic Palette: Phrygian-dominant scales, intentionally detuned oscillators, and dissonant, bit-crushed chord clusters.
*   Production Signatures: "Thermal clipping" (intentionally heating circuits to achieve specific harmonic saturation), sample-rate degradation applied to real-time feedback loops, and physically recorded field recordings of sandstorms layered under snare hits.
*   Dynamics: A rapid oscillation between absolute, suffocating density and sudden, eerie pin-drop silences that reveal the wind.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the Dust-Wastes where the Great Grid failed, the Rust-Baroque scene comprises tribes of "Signal-Walkers" who traverse the dunes on solar-rigged sand-crawlers. They wear layered nomadic wraps festooned with salvaged copper wiring and decommissioned circuit boards, using discarded Moog Minimoogs as portable shrines. Their central ritual, The Calibration, involves chaining multiple synthesizers together until they short-circuit, creating a fleeting, deafening harmonic bloom before the machine dies—a sacred sacrifice for the desert air.

Central Instruments: Salvaged Moog Minimoog (warped via battery-acid corrosive exposure), custom 8-bit gameboy-graft guitars, bowed copper piping, and salvaged server-fan percussion.

For Fans Of: Death Grips, 100 Gecs, Yellow Magic Orchestra, The Damned.

